1
00:00:00,491 --> 00:00:05,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is a verse from the Theradātta, and it's...

2
00:00:06,615 --> 00:00:17,403
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's 16.5, verses of 16, quotes from Malankīya Puttā, Arahant Malankīya Puttā.

3
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:32,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Having seen sights, recollectedness is forgotten when attending to the sign of the beloved.

4
00:00:33,971 --> 00:00:37,834
[SPEAKER_00]: He experiences an impassioned mind and he keeps on holding.

5
00:00:39,176 --> 00:00:43,240
[SPEAKER_00]: For him, with the arising of sights, various feelings increase.

6
00:00:44,435 --> 00:00:47,716
[SPEAKER_00]: For him, the mind is spoiled by desire and irritation.

7
00:00:48,856 --> 00:00:53,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Accumulating suffering in this way, nibbāna is said to be far away.

8
00:00:54,557 --> 00:01:08,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it goes on, having heard a sound, smelled a smell, tasted a taste, touched a touch, and having known a phenomenon.

9
00:01:14,323 --> 00:01:22,607
[SPEAKER_00]: I love it.

10
00:01:22,907 --> 00:01:36,474
[SPEAKER_02]: Beautiful.

11
00:01:36,514 --> 00:01:37,355
[SPEAKER_02]: Is this your translation?

12
00:01:38,035 --> 00:01:38,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Nice.

13
00:01:39,756 --> 00:01:43,598
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how would you... what do you think, having seen sites... Well, first of all,

14
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:45,781
[SPEAKER_02]: Who do you think this applies to?

15
00:01:47,922 --> 00:01:50,964
[SPEAKER_00]: This structure?

16
00:01:51,504 --> 00:02:00,250
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking it might apply to some noble disciple who's not practicing.

17
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:01,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

18
00:02:02,031 --> 00:02:04,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's losing it.

19
00:02:04,092 --> 00:02:05,373
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he's not maintaining it.

20
00:02:05,393 --> 00:02:06,473
[SPEAKER_02]: The right recollection.

21
00:02:06,573 --> 00:02:06,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

22
00:02:08,338 --> 00:02:10,700
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, for him, nibbāna is far away.

23
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:14,423
[SPEAKER_02]: But, he still can get nibbāna, if he were to do it rightly.

24
00:02:15,063 --> 00:02:17,185
[SPEAKER_02]: For Kutujana, nibbāna is invisible.

25
00:02:17,285 --> 00:02:19,106
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not even far away.

26
00:02:19,126 --> 00:02:19,867
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just not there.

27
00:02:21,468 --> 00:02:23,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Recollectedness is forgotten.

28
00:02:23,609 --> 00:02:24,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

29
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:25,411
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows what recollectedness is.

30
00:02:25,431 --> 00:02:28,073
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows what recollectedness is, what the right sati is.

31
00:02:28,133 --> 00:02:30,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

32
00:02:32,556 --> 00:02:35,919
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and how about you, when he says various feelings increase?

33
00:02:36,759 --> 00:02:37,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Increase in what?

34
00:02:40,167 --> 00:02:41,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Increasing the pressure.

35
00:02:42,189 --> 00:02:42,389
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

36
00:02:42,610 --> 00:02:43,811
[SPEAKER_02]: And how is the pressure felt?

37
00:02:46,914 --> 00:02:49,676
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you feel the pressure of the feeling in the past?

38
00:02:52,399 --> 00:02:53,440
[SPEAKER_02]: It's felt presently.

39
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:53,900
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

40
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:54,661
[SPEAKER_02]: But...

41
00:02:54,681 --> 00:02:56,363
[SPEAKER_02]: So, increasing in the in the presence.

42
00:02:57,143 --> 00:02:58,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Feelings increase in the presence.

43
00:02:59,365 --> 00:02:59,926
[SPEAKER_02]: In the pressure.

44
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:00,146
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

45
00:03:00,206 --> 00:03:00,867
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like some

46
00:03:02,119 --> 00:03:02,699
[SPEAKER_02]: It is pressure.

47
00:03:02,719 --> 00:03:04,000
[SPEAKER_02]: You're always pressured by the feeling.

48
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:04,620
[SPEAKER_02]: But, eh...

49
00:03:05,601 --> 00:03:10,383
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it's even more accurate to say that they increase in their presence, in how much they're present for you.

50
00:03:11,403 --> 00:03:16,625
[SPEAKER_02]: Because people tend to already interpret feelings as kind of energies and sensations.

51
00:03:17,486 --> 00:03:23,568
[SPEAKER_02]: And saying them increase means, means that the energetic pull of your feeling might be increasing or something.

52
00:03:23,728 --> 00:03:24,949
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is kind of true.

53
00:03:25,529 --> 00:03:27,430
[SPEAKER_02]: But really, they're increasing in their presence.

54
00:03:28,828 --> 00:03:31,589
[SPEAKER_02]: How much of that presence is over you?

55
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:35,151
[SPEAKER_02]: And the more you give in, the more it becomes.

56
00:03:36,012 --> 00:03:43,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you think of it in terms of the, um... the... that... the arrow, Sutta, you know, the, um... the second arrow?

57
00:03:45,036 --> 00:03:45,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no.

58
00:03:46,037 --> 00:03:46,277
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

59
00:03:46,577 --> 00:03:47,838
[SPEAKER_02]: Because this is for an Arjuna.

60
00:03:48,698 --> 00:03:49,999
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't get the second arrow.

61
00:03:50,159 --> 00:03:50,679
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he doesn't.

62
00:03:50,859 --> 00:03:51,059
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

63
00:03:51,139 --> 00:03:51,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

64
00:03:52,625 --> 00:03:56,928
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's literally, like, feelings will increase when you attend to them wrongly.

65
00:03:56,948 --> 00:04:03,953
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, they will be more... more of... they will have more of their presence in that pulling wrong manner.

66
00:04:04,373 --> 00:04:06,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, you're scratching your itch.

67
00:04:06,735 --> 00:04:08,776
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're not scratching it, the itch will start to fade.

68
00:04:08,816 --> 00:04:11,058
[SPEAKER_02]: You start scratching it, it starts to itch more.

69
00:04:11,258 --> 00:04:12,999
[SPEAKER_02]: It's increasing in presence of the itch.

70
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:14,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

71
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:14,981
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing like...

72
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:23,404
[SPEAKER_02]: Future repercussions of your wrong attending now, an accumulation of this... No, it's literally, you're doing it wrongly, you're going to feel it more.

73
00:04:24,405 --> 00:04:24,745
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

74
00:04:25,165 --> 00:04:26,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feelings are more of an issue.

75
00:04:27,246 --> 00:04:27,647
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

76
00:04:27,787 --> 00:04:30,648
[SPEAKER_02]: More pressing, more pressuring, more of a burden.

77
00:04:31,309 --> 00:04:33,930
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because you're scratching them, and that's making things worse.

78
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:35,431
[SPEAKER_02]: You stop scratching, they're going to get better.

79
00:04:36,191 --> 00:04:39,493
[SPEAKER_02]: But, this Sutta, these verses say exactly that.

80
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:42,255
[SPEAKER_02]: By wrongly attending to it, they increase.

81
00:04:43,215 --> 00:04:46,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Wrongly attending to the beloved... To the arising of the sights.

82
00:04:46,997 --> 00:04:47,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

83
00:04:47,217 --> 00:04:47,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Losing the context.

84
00:04:47,998 --> 00:04:51,279
[SPEAKER_02]: Losing perspective on account of the scene.

85
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:53,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Losing perspective on account of the heard.

86
00:04:54,221 --> 00:04:57,222
[SPEAKER_02]: So, one looks at the attractive part.

87
00:04:58,123 --> 00:05:00,764
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it happens before you look at the attractive.

88
00:05:01,345 --> 00:05:05,687
[SPEAKER_02]: When the attractive comes within your range, you have already picked the sign of the attractive and then you choose to look.

89
00:05:07,345 --> 00:05:12,669
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you wouldn't... you cannot look at the attractive accidentally, because it's not attractive at that time yet.

90
00:05:13,529 --> 00:05:18,833
[SPEAKER_02]: You can look at accidentally at something, then you realize it was attractive, and then, that's the time when you have a choice.

91
00:05:19,893 --> 00:05:20,814
[SPEAKER_02]: Shall I look again?

92
00:05:21,835 --> 00:05:22,755
[SPEAKER_02]: Or have a better look?

93
00:05:23,436 --> 00:05:26,078
[SPEAKER_02]: Or shall I refrain from looking?

94
00:05:26,158 --> 00:05:27,439
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what this refers to.

95
00:05:27,499 --> 00:05:32,542
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you choose then to attend wrongly, as in you choose to look again.

96
00:05:34,263 --> 00:05:36,885
[SPEAKER_02]: Although you know that now you perceive it as attractive.

97
00:05:38,533 --> 00:05:44,354
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the context of attractiveness has arisen in the mind, and now you act out of it, and that's your responsibility.

98
00:05:45,854 --> 00:05:52,475
[SPEAKER_02]: And for as long as you keep doing that, the context of attractiveness will remain lingering around you, because you're maintaining it.

99
00:05:52,715 --> 00:05:57,316
[SPEAKER_01]: And your mind will continue to... And your mind will be far away from nibbāna.

100
00:05:58,236 --> 00:06:00,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, you said, recollectiveness is forgotten.

101
00:06:00,877 --> 00:06:07,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather than just being, just, you know, mindfulness, just, you know, like...

102
00:06:09,183 --> 00:06:10,365
[SPEAKER_01]: General mindfulness.

103
00:06:11,967 --> 00:06:19,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, can we say it's being recollected, recollecting a particular thing?

104
00:06:21,481 --> 00:06:24,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, maybe in this case, body.

105
00:06:26,845 --> 00:06:27,305
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, I see.

106
00:06:27,785 --> 00:06:29,866
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not actually particular, in that sense.

107
00:06:30,246 --> 00:06:31,026
[SPEAKER_02]: No, not particular.

108
00:06:31,046 --> 00:06:32,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I see what you mean.

109
00:06:32,307 --> 00:06:33,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather than just saying, oh, I'm just being mindful.

110
00:06:34,367 --> 00:06:35,108
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yes, of course.

111
00:06:35,188 --> 00:06:37,608
[SPEAKER_02]: No, there always has to be a reference point for reflectiveness.

112
00:06:37,628 --> 00:06:38,288
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean, yeah.

113
00:06:38,689 --> 00:06:41,289
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm just mindful of here and now, present moment.

114
00:06:41,349 --> 00:06:41,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

115
00:06:42,110 --> 00:06:43,490
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you're not mindful of anything.

116
00:06:43,550 --> 00:06:43,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

117
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:49,592
[SPEAKER_01]: But in this case, it seems, because he's talking about the senses.

118
00:06:51,352 --> 00:06:52,293
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, yeah, sure.

119
00:06:52,693 --> 00:06:53,293
[SPEAKER_02]: And obviously, the

120
00:06:54,065 --> 00:06:55,589
[SPEAKER_02]: That's kind of the only mindfulness you can do.

121
00:06:55,949 --> 00:06:58,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Even mindfulness of the thoughts, again.

122
00:07:03,755 --> 00:07:06,136
[SPEAKER_02]: It would reveal the body being there as a necessary basis.

123
00:07:07,277 --> 00:07:07,597
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

124
00:07:07,977 --> 00:07:08,978
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it would have to be there.

125
00:07:09,038 --> 00:07:09,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

126
00:07:09,738 --> 00:07:11,819
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's to mean, like, it's losing the context.

127
00:07:12,159 --> 00:07:12,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

128
00:07:12,599 --> 00:07:15,281
[SPEAKER_02]: In regard to the seeing, heard, smell, taste, touch and so on.

129
00:07:15,781 --> 00:07:20,243
[SPEAKER_02]: The context being the body given there beforehand as a as a container for these things to be.

130
00:07:20,743 --> 00:07:26,346
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is no problem in in things presenting themselves as attractive or as repulsive.

131
00:07:26,906 --> 00:07:28,646
[SPEAKER_02]: If you see it correctly on the basis of the body.

132
00:07:29,267 --> 00:07:38,189
[SPEAKER_02]: You see that on the basis of the body that's not mine, things present themselves as attractive, as repulsive, as neutral, which are equally not mine, because they're on the basis of the body.

133
00:07:38,209 --> 00:07:39,009
[SPEAKER_02]: They cannot be in me.

134
00:07:39,869 --> 00:07:46,291
[SPEAKER_02]: You forgetting the basis of the body, you and attractive, or you and repulsive, or you and neutral, form a relationship.

135
00:07:48,031 --> 00:07:49,471
[SPEAKER_02]: And even that's not necessarily...

136
00:07:49,491 --> 00:07:52,492
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, that's a problem, but that's a problem that you have to basically uproot.

137
00:07:53,072 --> 00:07:56,293
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't just say no to it and stop doing that, because that's still not you doing.

138
00:07:57,495 --> 00:08:16,386
[SPEAKER_02]: But once you form the relationship with with the scene, on account of its attraction, or on account of its repulsiveness, like, oh, I hate that, eh... that's, eh... when you... when you choose then to act out of that relationship, that's... that's how you perpetuate the problem, and fuel it, and make the feelings increase, as a result of it.

139
00:08:17,506 --> 00:08:21,488
[SPEAKER_02]: So, next time, the pressure of relationship would be even... even... even heavier on you.

140
00:08:22,149 --> 00:08:23,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's... it's harder to bear.

141
00:08:24,772 --> 00:08:24,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

142
00:08:25,212 --> 00:08:25,873
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

143
00:08:26,273 --> 00:08:27,034
[SPEAKER_02]: It's harder to bear.

144
00:08:27,254 --> 00:08:29,616
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feelings become harder to bear.

145
00:08:29,756 --> 00:08:29,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

146
00:08:30,197 --> 00:08:31,838
[SPEAKER_02]: As opposed to... Because you were giving in to them.

147
00:08:32,178 --> 00:08:33,319
[SPEAKER_00]: If you didn't, they weren't.

148
00:08:33,559 --> 00:08:36,181
[SPEAKER_02]: They would be less and less and less hard to bear.

149
00:08:36,221 --> 00:08:36,562
[SPEAKER_02]: Burdensome.

150
00:08:36,702 --> 00:08:37,663
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, less burdensome.

151
00:08:38,123 --> 00:08:38,984
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they would...

152
00:08:39,404 --> 00:08:47,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then... As you were saying earlier, an arahant would not be burdened by feeling.

153
00:08:47,291 --> 00:08:47,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

154
00:08:47,471 --> 00:08:49,112
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's said he wouldn't feel anything.

155
00:08:49,352 --> 00:08:50,033
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't feel.

156
00:08:50,253 --> 00:08:51,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling is the burden.

157
00:08:53,388 --> 00:08:54,889
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like some feelings are a burden.

158
00:08:54,929 --> 00:08:55,069
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

159
00:08:55,269 --> 00:08:56,710
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling in its nature is a burden.

160
00:08:57,190 --> 00:08:59,971
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling towards attractive, feeling towards repulsive, feeling towards neutral.

161
00:08:59,991 --> 00:09:00,912
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a burden either way.

162
00:09:00,932 --> 00:09:02,272
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you are subjected to it.

163
00:09:02,373 --> 00:09:02,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

164
00:09:03,613 --> 00:09:04,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's upon you.

165
00:09:04,994 --> 00:09:08,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't get... as an arahant, you wouldn't be subjected anymore.

166
00:09:08,956 --> 00:09:09,576
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't feel.

167
00:09:09,596 --> 00:09:09,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

168
00:09:09,776 --> 00:09:10,817
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't have any pressure.

169
00:09:10,837 --> 00:09:13,258
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what then happens to feeling?

170
00:09:14,339 --> 00:09:18,821
[SPEAKER_00]: As an aggregate, what is the feeling to you?

171
00:09:21,142 --> 00:09:23,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's that which is not for Patujan.

172
00:09:24,661 --> 00:09:30,486
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot possibly compare the aggregate of feeling for an arahant and for puthujjana from an impersonal standpoint.

173
00:09:31,407 --> 00:09:33,289
[SPEAKER_02]: You're either closer to one or the other.

174
00:09:37,572 --> 00:09:41,476
[SPEAKER_02]: So the aggregate of feeling for an arahant is the same what it is for puthujjana.

175
00:09:42,316 --> 00:09:44,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Something out there that's not felt nor can it be owned.

176
00:09:45,699 --> 00:09:50,744
[SPEAKER_02]: However, puthujjana is pressured by that thing out there which cannot be felt nor owned.

177
00:09:51,607 --> 00:09:54,408
[SPEAKER_02]: And because of that pressure, he feels it, he owns it.

178
00:09:56,609 --> 00:10:01,091
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the aggregate of feeling for an arahant is what the aggregate of feeling is.

179
00:10:01,111 --> 00:10:04,432
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not the aggregate of putujana that's the actual aggregate of feeling.

180
00:10:04,452 --> 00:10:06,133
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a mistake in regard to the aggregates.

181
00:10:07,153 --> 00:10:08,314
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the state of putujana.

182
00:10:09,374 --> 00:10:12,976
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not that you... Like, in practice, you have to start from putujana to an arahant.

183
00:10:13,456 --> 00:10:17,678
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you want to look theoretically on the structure, it's the structure of the arahant's aggregates.

184
00:10:17,698 --> 00:10:18,478
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what's first.

185
00:10:19,313 --> 00:10:20,194
[SPEAKER_02]: That has always been like that.

186
00:10:20,214 --> 00:10:20,894
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what Buddha said.

187
00:10:21,495 --> 00:10:24,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Upādāna is not in the aggregates, cannot take the aggregates.

188
00:10:24,797 --> 00:10:27,039
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not part of them, and it's not away from them either.

189
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:35,665
[SPEAKER_02]: It's desire and lust in regard to the aggregates, including the aggregated feeling, that these aggregates, that the upādāna is there, that your assumption, your attachment is there.

190
00:10:37,627 --> 00:10:38,247
[SPEAKER_02]: I was thinking that...

191
00:10:38,267 --> 00:10:41,630
[SPEAKER_02]: So, really, you should ask, what is the aggregated feeling for a bhutujjana?

192
00:10:41,910 --> 00:10:45,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Clarify that, and you'll have the aggregate of an arahant.

193
00:10:46,454 --> 00:11:01,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, hmm, let me get to the theoretical point of aggregate for an arhant, because you'll be arriving at it through being subjected to the pressure of the feeling that you already have, which means you won't be arriving at it correctly.

194
00:11:02,263 --> 00:11:06,125
[SPEAKER_01]: To talk about two different types of aggregates, that's actually what the Buddha does.

195
00:11:06,246 --> 00:11:12,369
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, there's the khandhas and there's the upādānakhandhā, pañcupādānakkhandhā.

196
00:11:12,929 --> 00:11:14,390
[SPEAKER_02]: But I don't want to do that.

197
00:11:16,582 --> 00:11:17,723
[SPEAKER_02]: Without qualifying it.

198
00:11:17,883 --> 00:11:18,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

199
00:11:18,143 --> 00:11:19,804
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's already misunderstood enough.

200
00:11:20,244 --> 00:11:20,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

201
00:11:21,505 --> 00:11:29,969
[SPEAKER_02]: Because many people would think, I'm a pūtujana, I read the sutta, and pretend to be an arahant, and I get the idea of it, and that idea stands for that which arahant is.

202
00:11:30,090 --> 00:11:32,791
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, as a pūtujana, I understand what the arahant is.

203
00:11:34,052 --> 00:11:38,454
[SPEAKER_02]: And now I just have to adhere to my method, or technique, or practice, and I'll magically arrive at it.

204
00:11:38,914 --> 00:11:43,097
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you cannot understand what an arahant is, which is why you're still a pūtujana.

205
00:11:45,095 --> 00:11:48,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding what an arahant is, you cannot be able to judge.

206
00:11:48,457 --> 00:12:02,426
[SPEAKER_00]: So you have feelings now, and then if you lose the assumption in regard to the feeling now, does that feeling disappear?

207
00:12:02,446 --> 00:12:06,068
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that's a wrong question, because you don't lose assumption in regard to the feeling.

208
00:12:06,928 --> 00:12:08,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Your assumption is your feeling.

209
00:12:09,270 --> 00:12:10,951
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no feeling left or right from it.

210
00:12:12,564 --> 00:12:14,585
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you trying to separate it is impossible.

211
00:12:14,945 --> 00:12:18,285
[SPEAKER_02]: The only way to separate your assumption from your feeling is to become an arahant.

212
00:12:20,626 --> 00:12:23,487
[SPEAKER_02]: So, asking them, what happens to feeling when my assumption ceases?

213
00:12:24,307 --> 00:12:26,608
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot conceive for as long as your assumption is still there.

214
00:12:27,088 --> 00:12:29,148
[SPEAKER_02]: Because your feeling is your assumption.

215
00:12:29,628 --> 00:12:31,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Your aggregates are your assumption.

216
00:12:31,589 --> 00:12:35,430
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's said they're taken up, wrongly, but those are the only ones you know.

217
00:12:36,428 --> 00:12:39,108
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the same question would be wrong to ask for any aggregate.

218
00:12:39,128 --> 00:12:41,789
[SPEAKER_02]: What happens to consciousness when there is no upādāna?

219
00:12:42,569 --> 00:12:44,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Your upādāna is your consciousness.

220
00:12:44,729 --> 00:12:46,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Your upādāna is pañcupādānakhandhā.

221
00:12:49,730 --> 00:12:54,771
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as this feeling ceases... You say, eh... upādāna ceases.

222
00:12:57,712 --> 00:13:05,193
[SPEAKER_02]: So, putujana's feeling, which is feeling of vedanā upādāna, it completely doesn't exist in our mind.

223
00:13:06,607 --> 00:13:07,148
[SPEAKER_00]: It has ceased.

224
00:13:07,748 --> 00:13:08,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite vague, in a sense.

225
00:13:08,549 --> 00:13:11,091
[SPEAKER_00]: It has ceased.

226
00:13:11,111 --> 00:13:12,172
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't exist, I mean.

227
00:13:12,492 --> 00:13:12,993
[SPEAKER_02]: It has ceased.

228
00:13:13,073 --> 00:13:13,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything has ceased.

229
00:13:13,814 --> 00:13:16,536
[SPEAKER_02]: The five... pañcupādānakkhandhā has ceased for an arahant.

230
00:13:19,379 --> 00:13:23,463
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's important to realize that the only way to understand, then, what's left is to become an arahant.

231
00:13:24,523 --> 00:13:28,467
[SPEAKER_02]: Not to theoretically compare pañcupādānakkhandhā with pañcakhandhā and then get the idea.

232
00:13:28,567 --> 00:13:32,491
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can get the idea, theoretical, but don't confuse it as understanding it for what it is.

233
00:13:33,747 --> 00:13:40,090
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, see, it can result in that kind of subtle, but yet quite profound and fundamental separation.

234
00:13:40,610 --> 00:13:45,192
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, upādāna and my assumption... vedanā and my assumption in regard to my vedanā.

235
00:13:45,672 --> 00:13:45,812
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

236
00:13:46,052 --> 00:13:47,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Your vedanā is your upādāna.

237
00:13:48,333 --> 00:13:49,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Your aggregates are your upādāna.

238
00:13:51,194 --> 00:13:54,176
[SPEAKER_02]: The container for putujana is upādāna, of everything.

239
00:13:54,756 --> 00:13:59,578
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can measure your upādāna in regard to how you feel.

240
00:14:00,533 --> 00:14:04,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you can measure the the the amount of upādāna in regard to how much you're liable to suffer.

241
00:14:05,838 --> 00:14:08,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, but if feelings of... if you're pressured by... Sure.

242
00:14:09,021 --> 00:14:10,182
[SPEAKER_02]: That means the amount of pressure.

243
00:14:10,242 --> 00:14:10,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

244
00:14:10,782 --> 00:14:10,942
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

245
00:14:11,062 --> 00:14:11,403
[SPEAKER_00]: There is.

246
00:14:11,423 --> 00:14:12,163
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you can say that.

247
00:14:12,283 --> 00:14:12,464
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

248
00:14:13,645 --> 00:14:15,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not being pressured at all.

249
00:14:16,427 --> 00:14:18,869
[SPEAKER_02]: Not being pressured at all means not not holding it up.

250
00:14:19,570 --> 00:14:20,471
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no upādāna there.

251
00:14:21,231 --> 00:14:23,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Which you could also say is not feeling.

252
00:14:24,574 --> 00:14:25,595
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not felt, exactly.

253
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:29,540
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

254
00:14:29,560 --> 00:14:32,061
[SPEAKER_02]: The feeling you feel is the feeling of upādāna.

255
00:14:34,621 --> 00:14:36,742
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can there be a feeling without being felt?

256
00:14:37,042 --> 00:14:37,942
[SPEAKER_02]: You become an arahant.

257
00:14:38,142 --> 00:14:38,562
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how.

258
00:14:39,983 --> 00:14:47,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when an ārya-sārvaka says it quite explicitly, when an ārya-sārvaka, when a noble disciple practices, he exhausts feeling.

259
00:14:47,745 --> 00:14:49,145
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't accumulate feeling.

260
00:14:49,165 --> 00:14:49,925
[SPEAKER_02]: He exhausts it.

261
00:14:50,265 --> 00:14:50,805
[SPEAKER_02]: He wipes it away.

262
00:14:50,825 --> 00:14:54,226
[SPEAKER_02]: And accumulate means he doesn't, yeah, increase through scratching carelessly.

263
00:14:54,586 --> 00:14:55,367
[SPEAKER_02]: It will itch more.

264
00:14:55,487 --> 00:14:56,827
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he's not accumulating that.

265
00:14:57,750 --> 00:15:01,993
[SPEAKER_02]: He stops scratching, stops itching, heals, and then he does not itch at all.

266
00:15:02,554 --> 00:15:04,095
[SPEAKER_02]: There is nothing felt anymore.

267
00:15:06,417 --> 00:15:16,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he... he is not impassioned by sights.

268
00:15:17,405 --> 00:15:19,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Having seen a sight, he is recollected.

269
00:15:20,816 --> 00:15:23,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Having seen the sight, the context remains imperturbed.

270
00:15:24,338 --> 00:15:29,841
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he does not lose the sight of the body being the container of whatever scene, pleasant or pleasant-neutral, doesn't matter.

271
00:15:30,341 --> 00:15:40,406
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he doesn't forsake that image for going towards the pleasant sight, which is what definition of passion is.

272
00:15:43,308 --> 00:15:47,510
[SPEAKER_00]: He experiences a dispassionate mind and he doesn't keep on holding

273
00:15:49,199 --> 00:15:54,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's impossible to hold in the first place, and he does not forget that.

274
00:15:54,441 --> 00:16:00,924
[SPEAKER_00]: For one, seeing a sight like this, while also practicing feeling, is exhausted, not accumulated.

275
00:16:01,824 --> 00:16:02,804
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives like this.

276
00:16:03,004 --> 00:16:03,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

277
00:16:03,785 --> 00:16:06,266
[SPEAKER_02]: Presence of the feeling fades away, does not increase.

278
00:16:08,506 --> 00:16:12,348
[SPEAKER_02]: Until it fades away completely, you can say it's ceased and cannot arise.

279
00:16:14,622 --> 00:16:20,328
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, diminishing suffering in this way, nibbāna is said to be near.

280
00:16:22,290 --> 00:16:26,995
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he diminishes... His liability to suffering.

281
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:36,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all he's doing, and Aryan is doing, is just...

282
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:40,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keeping that recollection and...

283
00:16:40,202 --> 00:16:40,763
[SPEAKER_02]: Restraining?

284
00:16:41,484 --> 00:16:46,929
[SPEAKER_02]: If there are things, yeah, if there are things that are sort of trying to induce the scratch, all he needs to do is restrain.

285
00:16:48,070 --> 00:16:53,595
[SPEAKER_02]: Because restraint, for me, for him, would mean maintenance of the right context that he has obtained by becoming a sotāpanna.

286
00:16:57,118 --> 00:17:00,561
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, for Putrajaya, you need a bit more than to just... yeah, you need to restrain.

287
00:17:00,581 --> 00:17:01,562
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a necessary basis.

288
00:17:01,923 --> 00:17:04,305
[SPEAKER_02]: But you also need to then understand what the right context is.

289
00:17:05,993 --> 00:17:21,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, you can restrain as much as you want, but you still remain holding on to the wrong context, not realizing the relationship that whatever seen is within the necessary base of the body that you are mindful of, and including your own sense of self, is within the necessary base of the body that you are mindful of.

290
00:17:22,446 --> 00:17:27,090
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is no external to the necessary base of the body that you are recollecting correctly.

291
00:17:28,413 --> 00:17:30,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Fully dwell on that as a patujana, that there is no external.

292
00:17:31,675 --> 00:17:40,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Once you go through the few pangs of anxiety and fear that you will drown and it will kill you, you get used to it, you stop resisting it, and then you find it liberating.

293
00:17:43,223 --> 00:17:51,668
[SPEAKER_02]: How much it will bother you, that depends how good your sīla is established, how much pressure you carry anyway, how many habits of scratching you carry and you have to restrain.

294
00:17:52,168 --> 00:17:54,490
[SPEAKER_02]: So it doesn't need to always be unpleasant, but I think

295
00:17:55,268 --> 00:17:59,069
[SPEAKER_02]: More often than not, today, I think it's going to be more unpleasant than not in the beginning.

296
00:18:00,090 --> 00:18:05,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Because more... more often than not, people are much more into the senses than they used to be.

297
00:18:07,372 --> 00:18:08,212
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the decline.

298
00:18:09,672 --> 00:18:12,633
[SPEAKER_02]: The threshold of your being is already far, far proliferated.

299
00:18:13,013 --> 00:18:20,216
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, you know, even in the... as you were speaking earlier, like, even, you know, even the underprivileged in Western societies, they're extremely privileged.

300
00:18:20,796 --> 00:18:22,056
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't go hungry or something.

301
00:18:22,076 --> 00:18:24,177
[SPEAKER_02]: You might not be able to get fashionable things and...

302
00:18:26,221 --> 00:18:32,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Some kind of items of entertainment and whatnot, but far from that you are actually starving or not being able to eat.

303
00:18:33,925 --> 00:18:38,088
[SPEAKER_02]: On the contrary, I mean, most people have weight problems, certainly in Western Europe.

304
00:18:39,009 --> 00:18:42,491
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it just tells you, like, where... where the starting point is.

305
00:18:43,151 --> 00:18:52,457
[SPEAKER_02]: But back in the day, even the affluent people would still worry about, you know, next month the king might come and kill all of my family members and take all of my possessions.

306
00:18:52,497 --> 00:18:53,978
[SPEAKER_02]: And you leave that with the back of your mind.

307
00:18:56,237 --> 00:18:58,038
[SPEAKER_02]: And you just have to accept it.

308
00:18:58,058 --> 00:19:00,018
[SPEAKER_02]: And every day has that perspective on it.

309
00:19:00,619 --> 00:19:07,041
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, yeah, my little mud hut with my six children, in the slightest earthquake, the thing might fall and kill us all at night.

310
00:19:07,941 --> 00:19:13,803
[SPEAKER_02]: And you get used to it, so you don't necessarily feel the... that kind of intense urgency of it, but it doesn't mean it's not there.

311
00:19:14,323 --> 00:19:14,663
[SPEAKER_02]: It's there.

312
00:19:14,703 --> 00:19:15,384
[SPEAKER_02]: You live under it.

313
00:19:17,204 --> 00:19:18,144
[SPEAKER_02]: And today people don't.

314
00:19:18,785 --> 00:19:19,205
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the...

315
00:19:19,825 --> 00:19:25,487
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean... You might worry about somebody says something that upsets you, existentially,

316
00:19:26,279 --> 00:19:27,060
[SPEAKER_02]: It kind of shanks you.

317
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:29,542
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's far from any actual threat.

318
00:19:32,764 --> 00:19:36,888
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's the starting point, which means that needs to be undone.

319
00:19:36,908 --> 00:19:41,791
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what I mean, like, in present day, if somebody tries to undo that, it's going to be unpleasant initially.

320
00:19:42,292 --> 00:19:44,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're far out there in the senses.

321
00:19:44,714 --> 00:19:50,919
[SPEAKER_02]: Depending on constant activity, constant engagement, constant distraction, constant input, constant everything.

322
00:19:54,270 --> 00:19:58,013
[SPEAKER_02]: Because in the same fashion, the more you scratch, the more it will itch.

323
00:19:59,334 --> 00:20:02,097
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you got used to the point of scratching at the click of a button.

324
00:20:02,817 --> 00:20:07,742
[SPEAKER_02]: Tap on a phone, phone call, company, food, food, food, eat five, six times a day.

325
00:20:08,482 --> 00:20:10,364
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, that becomes norm and necessity.

326
00:20:11,045 --> 00:20:16,650
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you can't sustain that level of engagement, you're gonna suffer, gonna counter it.

327
00:20:18,497 --> 00:20:28,322
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you don't go through that suffering of that withdrawal, well, you'll never arrive at this position whereby you can maintain the perspective and then see your choice in choosing to go that way or choosing not to go that way.

328
00:20:28,342 --> 00:20:29,583
[SPEAKER_02]: That's invisible for most people.

329
00:20:30,083 --> 00:20:35,426
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they say, oh, you know, like, essential desire is just nature, it's just natural, as in it has nothing to do with me.

330
00:20:36,486 --> 00:20:39,388
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that you can say no to it means it has everything to do with you.

331
00:20:40,288 --> 00:20:43,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever you can say no to, you're responsible for.

332
00:20:44,831 --> 00:20:45,091
[SPEAKER_02]: And so,

333
00:20:46,124 --> 00:20:49,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Revise that and see what are the things I can say no to.

334
00:20:50,348 --> 00:20:55,534
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't say no to your breathing, to your heart beating, to aging, to getting sick.

335
00:20:55,574 --> 00:20:56,695
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't say no to dying.

336
00:20:56,715 --> 00:20:57,396
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't say no.

337
00:20:57,696 --> 00:20:58,577
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's not for you.

338
00:20:59,558 --> 00:21:00,879
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't say no to hunger, sure.

339
00:21:01,420 --> 00:21:06,065
[SPEAKER_02]: You can literally say no to everything else, which means you're responsible for choosing to do everything else.

340
00:21:07,506 --> 00:21:08,147
[SPEAKER_02]: As simple as that.

341
00:21:12,753 --> 00:21:20,336
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, don't choose based on desire, don't choose based on your will, and don't choose based on desire to distract yourself, need to distract yourself.

342
00:21:21,396 --> 00:21:23,476
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you're getting ready to practice the dhamma.

343
00:21:31,199 --> 00:21:33,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you can say no to?

344
00:21:34,159 --> 00:21:35,200
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what you're responsible to.

345
00:21:43,027 --> 00:21:44,287
[SPEAKER_02]: You see, you can say no to eating.

346
00:21:44,487 --> 00:21:51,529
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't say no to needing food, but you can say no to needing a certain type of food.

347
00:21:52,569 --> 00:21:53,429
[SPEAKER_02]: Like when you have a choice.

348
00:21:54,629 --> 00:21:57,390
[SPEAKER_02]: You can say no to choosing on account of taste.

349
00:22:01,631 --> 00:22:04,631
[SPEAKER_02]: You can always say no to sexual intercourse.

350
00:22:04,651 --> 00:22:07,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why as Zananda said, it's not to be done.

351
00:22:07,792 --> 00:22:08,632
[SPEAKER_02]: It must be abandoned.

352
00:22:10,993 --> 00:22:12,513
[SPEAKER_02]: You can say no to acting out of anger.

353
00:22:14,121 --> 00:22:19,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter how angry you get, how upset you are, you choose to act out of it, which means you can say no to it.

354
00:22:19,442 --> 00:22:20,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you say no to feeling?

355
00:22:21,323 --> 00:22:21,483
No.

356
00:22:22,143 --> 00:22:22,563
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course not.

357
00:22:23,783 --> 00:22:30,264
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can say no to acting out of feeling, which is how feeling gets maintained and how feeling increases in its presence.

358
00:22:30,344 --> 00:22:36,266
[SPEAKER_02]: So, indirectly, you can say no to all of the five aggregates, which is what the nine hundred is.

359
00:22:36,286 --> 00:22:39,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Generally, people would think that would be some kind of repression.

360
00:22:39,606 --> 00:22:40,687
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

361
00:22:41,327 --> 00:22:42,267
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I say indirectly.

362
00:22:44,837 --> 00:22:46,079
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't repress things in there.

363
00:22:47,040 --> 00:22:50,224
[SPEAKER_02]: If you say no to this, that results in something else that you want to result in.

364
00:22:50,925 --> 00:22:54,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Repression is when you are like, no, no, no.

365
00:22:54,830 --> 00:22:57,013
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, sometimes you need to repress yourself.

366
00:22:57,754 --> 00:23:00,498
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to suppress your need to scratch like a maniac.

367
00:23:02,541 --> 00:23:06,304
[SPEAKER_02]: If your arms are itching, you have to stop scratching in order for them to heal.

368
00:23:06,905 --> 00:23:08,986
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, by all means, repress yourself.

369
00:23:09,307 --> 00:23:11,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Repress your anger.

370
00:23:12,329 --> 00:23:13,570
[SPEAKER_02]: What is better?

371
00:23:13,630 --> 00:23:16,693
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and even from the point of view of its repression...

372
00:23:17,273 --> 00:23:25,360
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you get angry in one instance, then you act out of it, and then the results of that, and harm, and whatever else, can cause further anger.

373
00:23:26,040 --> 00:23:29,063
[SPEAKER_02]: The other example gets angry, and then he represses it.

374
00:23:30,169 --> 00:23:31,990
[SPEAKER_02]: So, which is better, overall?

375
00:23:32,550 --> 00:23:34,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Both are affected by anger in the first place.

376
00:23:34,371 --> 00:23:37,533
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether you repress yourself or not, makes no difference.

377
00:23:37,593 --> 00:23:46,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, the one who is repressed, has not caused all this other load of suffering, and results, and repercussions of his actions.

378
00:23:46,137 --> 00:23:55,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, someone say, well, that person who repressed, now they're keeping it in, building it up, and now it's fermenting, and then they're gonna go and slaughter people.

379
00:23:55,161 --> 00:23:58,443
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if they do, it's because they haven't repressed themselves on that occasion.

380
00:23:59,905 --> 00:24:09,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the argument for celibacy, against celibacy.

381
00:24:09,916 --> 00:24:14,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you're keeping in your sexual desire, you keep it in, keep it in, and then you go and molest hundreds of children.

382
00:24:15,708 --> 00:24:17,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, those are two unrelated things.

383
00:24:17,169 --> 00:24:18,029
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's built up.

384
00:24:18,890 --> 00:24:25,533
[SPEAKER_02]: If you choose to go and molest children or do whatever else on account of that, it's because you chose to not repress yourself anymore.

385
00:24:26,114 --> 00:24:29,315
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you cannot say, I chose to not repress myself because of repression.

386
00:24:29,856 --> 00:24:30,956
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a contradiction in terms.

387
00:24:31,376 --> 00:24:33,558
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of repression, you would have stayed repressed.

388
00:24:34,198 --> 00:24:36,399
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, you would have been practicing sensory strain correctly.

389
00:24:36,919 --> 00:24:37,660
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're just weak.

390
00:24:38,320 --> 00:24:39,421
[SPEAKER_02]: You just could not see through.

391
00:24:39,941 --> 00:24:40,681
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the reason.

392
00:24:41,042 --> 00:24:41,522
[SPEAKER_02]: Your choice.

393
00:24:43,670 --> 00:24:46,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you didn't see what was causing all that anger.

394
00:24:46,331 --> 00:24:46,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

395
00:24:47,171 --> 00:24:47,671
[SPEAKER_02]: So, same.

396
00:24:47,731 --> 00:24:54,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Somebody who abstains from scratching their itch, then, when they give in, they're going to scratch twice as hard.

397
00:24:55,534 --> 00:24:57,094
[SPEAKER_02]: But there's nothing to do with their abstaining.

398
00:24:57,114 --> 00:25:00,115
[SPEAKER_02]: That's to do with their own weakness in regard to their abstaining.

399
00:25:01,395 --> 00:25:05,176
[SPEAKER_02]: To their own loss of perspective in regard to the abstaining.

400
00:25:05,537 --> 00:25:09,118
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if they haven't lost perspective of realizing that

401
00:25:12,150 --> 00:25:30,958
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you want to say the direct reason for molesting or doing crazy stuff on account of your repression, it's you losing perspective on account of your repression, not the repression.

402
00:25:32,358 --> 00:25:33,239
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, it's always on you.

403
00:25:33,619 --> 00:25:35,801
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter how much pressure it's on you to do it.

404
00:25:36,281 --> 00:25:39,283
[SPEAKER_02]: If you can say no to it, you will be responsible for doing it.

405
00:25:39,323 --> 00:25:44,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's this idea that you get you angry, you don't act on it.

406
00:25:44,708 --> 00:25:52,694
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, the person goes, goes away, but continues to brood over that whole thing, keeps on thinking about that, that anger.

407
00:25:52,914 --> 00:25:53,154
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

408
00:25:53,775 --> 00:25:54,615
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not doing anything.

409
00:25:54,635 --> 00:26:01,381
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so why, like, see, if that anger is happy, if that anger keeps brewing, what was the first point of anger?

410
00:26:02,141 --> 00:26:04,504
[SPEAKER_02]: Your repression or before your repression?

411
00:26:04,865 --> 00:26:06,046
[SPEAKER_02]: Where did the anger originate?

412
00:26:06,347 --> 00:26:07,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Before repression.

413
00:26:07,288 --> 00:26:09,711
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why do you blame repression for your anger brewing?

414
00:26:10,512 --> 00:26:13,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Can anger brew if it hasn't arisen in the first place?

415
00:26:15,418 --> 00:26:15,579
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

416
00:26:16,219 --> 00:26:19,464
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you repress anger if it hasn't arisen in the first place?

417
00:26:19,484 --> 00:26:19,644
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

418
00:26:21,805 --> 00:26:25,068
[SPEAKER_02]: So, your anger-brewing is not the result of your repression.

419
00:26:25,108 --> 00:26:27,070
[SPEAKER_02]: Your anger-brewing is the result of your anger.

420
00:26:28,111 --> 00:26:29,232
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the same thing is there.

421
00:26:29,272 --> 00:26:30,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

422
00:26:30,092 --> 00:26:32,775
[SPEAKER_00]: You just... you did repression, which is a good thing.

423
00:26:33,055 --> 00:26:34,176
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

424
00:26:34,196 --> 00:26:39,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Prevented you from... from, like, spilling it out there and affecting further domains, as opposed to just your mind.

425
00:26:40,402 --> 00:26:42,884
[SPEAKER_02]: You affected your verbal expression, you affected your physical...

426
00:26:42,904 --> 00:26:45,786
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't see... you're still cultivating the same... That's the same anger, yeah.

427
00:26:46,808 --> 00:26:48,491
[SPEAKER_02]: But now at least you have a chance of seeing it.

428
00:26:48,872 --> 00:26:58,410
[SPEAKER_02]: Once you depress your physical output and verbal output of acting out of anger, you have a chance to see that, ah, so it's still in my mind that same anger that I have restrained.

429
00:26:59,832 --> 00:27:08,195
[SPEAKER_02]: But you blaming restraint for brewing, means complete loss of perspective and seeing... failing to see that it brews because it was already there before.

430
00:27:08,355 --> 00:27:10,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why I practiced restraint in the first place.

431
00:27:10,895 --> 00:27:12,796
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing to restrain if the anger doesn't arise.

432
00:27:13,296 --> 00:27:16,317
[SPEAKER_00]: But then there's the... so I repress...

433
00:27:16,457 --> 00:27:18,178
[SPEAKER_00]: I repress the anger.

434
00:27:19,838 --> 00:27:22,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And then a person might have a view where it's still in me.

435
00:27:23,459 --> 00:27:25,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Now I must still get out in another way.

436
00:27:25,100 --> 00:27:26,100
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't act out of it.

437
00:27:28,127 --> 00:27:33,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, getting to kickboxing, because that's the healthy way to get out of anger.

438
00:27:33,309 --> 00:27:33,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

439
00:27:33,949 --> 00:27:35,929
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, there is no healthy way of getting out.

440
00:27:37,630 --> 00:27:39,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, you do the kickboxing.

441
00:27:39,410 --> 00:27:48,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, well, I think there is the infinite amount of magical connection that people can make if you want to try to avoid the responsibility for your risen state of mind.

442
00:27:50,314 --> 00:27:54,415
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether it's kickboxing, or finger painting, or slang poetry,

443
00:27:55,562 --> 00:27:57,083
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your magical belief that connects.

444
00:27:57,143 --> 00:27:59,004
[SPEAKER_02]: This is the outlet of my anger.

445
00:27:59,585 --> 00:28:00,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Or my sexual desire.

446
00:28:00,726 --> 00:28:01,226
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the same.

447
00:28:01,246 --> 00:28:01,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Painting, yeah.

448
00:28:01,927 --> 00:28:04,448
[SPEAKER_02]: You're responsible for it, for whatever you choose to do on account of it.

449
00:28:04,749 --> 00:28:17,958
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, finger painting is better than harming somebody physically, but you're still giving in to the need of acting out of anger, which means you're still affected by the anger, which means you're not going to uproot it.

450
00:28:21,507 --> 00:28:26,329
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes, but it is absolutely necessary that that wrong view be abandoned.

451
00:28:26,369 --> 00:28:28,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, these verses we read can start to apply.

452
00:28:29,371 --> 00:28:38,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you realize the responsibilities are new, never leaves you, and if you choose to go this direction, feeling will increase in its pressure and presence and burden.

453
00:28:39,075 --> 00:28:43,757
[SPEAKER_02]: If you choose to not go that direction, feeling will decrease in its pressure, presence and burden.

454
00:28:44,077 --> 00:28:45,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is closer to nibbāna.

455
00:28:45,718 --> 00:28:47,039
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is what nibbāna will be.

456
00:28:48,628 --> 00:28:50,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Rick, that's the measurement.

457
00:28:51,857 --> 00:28:56,710
[SPEAKER_00]: I get the feeling, the measurement for most people is, if I feel good,

458
00:28:59,130 --> 00:28:59,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

459
00:28:59,490 --> 00:29:00,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

460
00:29:00,531 --> 00:29:01,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

461
00:29:01,232 --> 00:29:02,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

462
00:29:02,292 --> 00:29:03,093
[SPEAKER_02]: It is.

463
00:29:03,133 --> 00:29:03,333
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

464
00:29:03,353 --> 00:29:04,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Practicing well.

465
00:29:04,554 --> 00:29:05,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything's going well.

466
00:29:05,734 --> 00:29:06,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

467
00:29:06,595 --> 00:29:09,036
[SPEAKER_00]: But feelings are increasing in that sense.

468
00:29:09,116 --> 00:29:09,997
[SPEAKER_00]: They're still increasing.

469
00:29:10,057 --> 00:29:11,117
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a pleasant feeling.

470
00:29:11,137 --> 00:29:12,598
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a pleasant feeling to increase.

471
00:29:13,659 --> 00:29:15,700
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's saying when feelings are exhausted.

472
00:29:15,940 --> 00:29:16,161
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

473
00:29:16,361 --> 00:29:17,061
[SPEAKER_02]: When nothing's felt.

474
00:29:17,101 --> 00:29:17,882
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what nibbāna is.

475
00:29:17,902 --> 00:29:19,583
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're feeling great and excited.

476
00:29:19,603 --> 00:29:20,603
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a problem.

477
00:29:20,623 --> 00:29:21,644
[SPEAKER_02]: You're far away from nibbāna.

478
00:29:21,664 --> 00:29:21,864
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

479
00:29:22,084 --> 00:29:24,325
[SPEAKER_02]: It's, it's, it's, well, it's, it's bigger problem than suffering.

480
00:29:25,246 --> 00:29:28,048
[SPEAKER_02]: As the Sutta says, he regards pain as a

481
00:29:29,482 --> 00:29:34,487
[SPEAKER_02]: Pleasure is a dart, and pleasure is a deadly arrow.

482
00:29:34,927 --> 00:29:39,071
[SPEAKER_02]: Pleasure is pain, and pain is a deadly arrow.

483
00:29:40,893 --> 00:29:42,014
[SPEAKER_02]: So, pain is even worse.

484
00:29:42,134 --> 00:29:42,994
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, pain, pain.

485
00:29:43,135 --> 00:29:44,476
[SPEAKER_02]: But pleasure, it's like the pain.

486
00:29:45,116 --> 00:29:48,700
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to regard that also as a dangerous and deadly in the same sense.

487
00:29:54,018 --> 00:29:55,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nothing is felt.

488
00:29:55,679 --> 00:29:56,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

489
00:29:56,859 --> 00:30:07,063
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the measure of your progress is the measure of the extent, how much you're not liable to feelings at all.

490
00:30:07,103 --> 00:30:07,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

491
00:30:07,543 --> 00:30:11,384
[SPEAKER_01]: And this, he says, he describes that... How much less you feel on account of this.

492
00:30:11,464 --> 00:30:14,686
[SPEAKER_01]: He describes that nothing being felt as being pleasant.

493
00:30:15,066 --> 00:30:16,686
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's pleasant in its own regard.

494
00:30:16,706 --> 00:30:18,227
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's a different kind of...

495
00:30:18,983 --> 00:30:22,090
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the type of pleasure you can understand by not feeling anything.

496
00:30:22,611 --> 00:30:26,279
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by, through feeling this pleasure, I get to understand what pleasure or non-feeling is.

497
00:30:27,521 --> 00:30:28,403
[SPEAKER_02]: That is impossible.

498
00:30:31,912 --> 00:30:35,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you read that, he doesn't feel anything.

499
00:30:35,193 --> 00:30:38,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing is felt.

500
00:30:38,135 --> 00:30:40,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people say, well, that's just depression.

501
00:30:41,496 --> 00:30:42,497
[SPEAKER_02]: For a putujana, it is.

502
00:30:42,857 --> 00:30:44,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Because for him, feeling is his existence.

503
00:30:45,098 --> 00:30:45,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling is his life.

504
00:30:45,898 --> 00:30:49,960
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they would freak out when the Buddha would describe nibbāna and they would understand what it is.

505
00:30:50,040 --> 00:30:52,461
[SPEAKER_02]: And they say, well, they will kill us, they will crush us, they will destroy me.

506
00:30:53,322 --> 00:30:53,962
[SPEAKER_02]: I will not be.

507
00:30:55,542 --> 00:30:57,103
[SPEAKER_02]: So, again, you have a choice.

508
00:30:57,123 --> 00:31:00,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I think, well... You can stay on the level of existence that you got accustomed to,

509
00:31:01,920 --> 00:31:02,901
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can't complain then.

510
00:31:04,222 --> 00:31:12,888
[SPEAKER_02]: When things don't go your way, when other people upset you, when you lose the loved ones, when you start losing your own health, life, youth, everything else, it's on you.

511
00:31:13,008 --> 00:31:14,929
[SPEAKER_02]: You chose to stay where these things apply.

512
00:31:17,291 --> 00:31:22,254
[SPEAKER_02]: You chose to hold on to it by constantly refusing to give up scratching of your itch.

513
00:31:25,496 --> 00:31:25,876
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

514
00:31:26,197 --> 00:31:27,878
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why beings are the owners of their actions.

515
00:31:28,836 --> 00:31:33,777
[SPEAKER_02]: Choices you make, they cannot possibly, like, be someone else's, the result of those things.

516
00:31:34,517 --> 00:31:40,278
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if people are born here, or reborn elsewhere, or reappear somewhere else, it's simply because of the choices they made.

517
00:31:41,999 --> 00:31:43,019
[SPEAKER_02]: And they continue to make.

518
00:31:44,819 --> 00:31:46,660
[SPEAKER_02]: So, sometimes people say, oh, you're lucky to be a monk.

519
00:31:47,660 --> 00:31:47,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

520
00:31:48,020 --> 00:31:49,060
[SPEAKER_02]: For choosing to be a monk?

521
00:31:49,280 --> 00:31:49,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

522
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:51,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I'm lucky inasmuch I'm still alive.

523
00:31:53,161 --> 00:31:54,421
[SPEAKER_02]: That's as far as the luck goes.

524
00:31:55,341 --> 00:31:56,522
[SPEAKER_02]: But I chose to be a monk.

525
00:31:57,522 --> 00:31:58,582
[SPEAKER_02]: I made the effort to be a monk.

526
00:31:59,518 --> 00:32:00,998
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're not a monk, that's on you.

527
00:32:01,759 --> 00:32:02,479
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, but I have family.

528
00:32:02,919 --> 00:32:04,379
[SPEAKER_02]: Has the family been thrown upon you?

529
00:32:05,640 --> 00:32:09,701
[SPEAKER_02]: Or have you produced it through sexual intercourse, through indulgence?

530
00:32:11,241 --> 00:32:13,682
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, so you're fully responsible to the things you could have said no.

531
00:32:14,162 --> 00:32:15,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you have a burden to weigh.

532
00:32:15,563 --> 00:32:15,983
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, sure.

533
00:32:16,023 --> 00:32:17,883
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now you can't because of what you chose to do.

534
00:32:19,244 --> 00:32:23,165
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you cannot give up the life and you want to give it up, whose fault is it?

535
00:32:23,665 --> 00:32:24,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Your own fault.

536
00:32:25,813 --> 00:32:27,656
[SPEAKER_02]: So, think wisely before you act.

537
00:32:28,577 --> 00:32:30,620
[SPEAKER_02]: It's never too late to start practicing that principle.

538
00:32:32,422 --> 00:32:34,265
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a payment you must make.

539
00:32:34,285 --> 00:32:37,189
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want nibbāna, you have to.

540
00:32:37,789 --> 00:32:39,111
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just get it for nothing.

541
00:32:39,332 --> 00:32:40,052
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

542
00:32:43,373 --> 00:32:46,454
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you know that thing that people quote Ajahn Chah.

543
00:32:46,494 --> 00:32:47,694
[SPEAKER_02]: It's quite accurate what he said.

544
00:32:48,035 --> 00:32:50,195
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, if you give up a little... Ajahn Chah said that.

545
00:32:50,275 --> 00:32:52,016
[SPEAKER_02]: If you give up a little, you get a little bit of peace.

546
00:32:52,876 --> 00:32:54,617
[SPEAKER_02]: If you give up a lot, you get a lot of peace.

547
00:32:54,677 --> 00:32:56,657
[SPEAKER_02]: If you give up everything, you get the ultimate peace.

548
00:32:56,957 --> 00:32:58,378
[SPEAKER_02]: Literally.

549
00:32:58,398 --> 00:33:01,039
[SPEAKER_02]: So, having things in peace are mutually exclusive.

550
00:33:01,119 --> 00:33:05,660
[SPEAKER_02]: Having things as in internally regarding them as yours, dear, beloved, and so on.

551
00:33:05,980 --> 00:33:08,741
[SPEAKER_02]: Having a right to them, a full entitlement to possess this, and so on.

552
00:33:09,735 --> 00:33:14,377
[SPEAKER_02]: So, giving up the ownership of things, to that extent, you're experiencing peace.

553
00:33:14,397 --> 00:33:19,058
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you don't want the bad feelings, but you want the good feelings, and it's this constant...

554
00:33:19,078 --> 00:33:24,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, normally, there's a constant trying to get away from that and just feel good, but you're just playing this...

555
00:33:24,440 --> 00:33:24,780
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

556
00:33:24,800 --> 00:33:27,361
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a very clever trap that Mahārāja sets up.

557
00:33:27,921 --> 00:33:36,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, see, you think you're making progress by avoiding the painful feeling on account of pleasant feeling, but you fail to see that either way you remain bound by the feelings, and that's what Mahārāja wants.

558
00:33:37,812 --> 00:33:43,874
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't care whether you suffer or you're pleasant, as long as you don't say no to feelings, as long as you don't step outside of his reach and his domain.

559
00:33:45,695 --> 00:33:55,699
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's why, whether you act out of pleasant feeling or unpleasant feeling, if you keep acting out of the pressure of the feeling, whatever the feeling is, means you will suffer and you will be bound by that.

560
00:33:56,880 --> 00:34:02,822
[SPEAKER_02]: So you can't have... nibbāna, you want at peace.

